Consider a process algebra in the style of ACP whose signature contains:
- a set A of atomic actions, together with a partial communication function γ : A × A → A that is commutative and associative;
- the constants a ∈ A;
- binary operators + (nondeterministic choice) and · (sequential composition);
- a binary parallel composition operator ‖ defined, as usual, in terms of the auxiliary left-merge ⌊⌊ and communication-merge | operators by the law

  x ‖ y = x ⌊⌊ y + y ⌊⌊ x + x | y,

with the standard operational semantics giving rise to (strong) bisimilarity ∼ on the set of closed terms (or, more generally, on open terms under the closed-instance interpretation).

An equational axiomatization E over this signature is sound for ∼ if every equation derivable from E holds modulo bisimilarity. It is ω-complete (also called inversely complete) if, for every equation t = u between open terms, E ⊢ t = u whenever every closed substitution instance σ(t) = σ(u) is derivable from E.

Problem. Exhibit an equational axiomatization E (using the signature above, possibly together with finitely many auxiliary operators such as left-merge and communication-merge) that is sound and ω-complete for bisimilarity over a process algebra with parallel composition based on synchronization in the style of ACP. In particular, determine whether such a finite ω-complete equational axiomatization exists, and if not, characterize the obstructions.

